Q: How can I format a number with commas as thousands separators? A: <p>My answer is the only answer that completely replaces jQuery with a much more sensible alternative:</p>
<pre><code>function $(dollarAmount)
{
    const locale = 'en-US';
    const options = { style: 'currency', currency: 'USD' };
    return Intl.NumberFormat(locale, options).format(dollarAmount);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This solution not only adds commas, but it also rounds to the nearest penny in the event that you input an amount like <code>$(1000.9999)</code> you'll get $1,001.00. Additionally, the value you input can safely be a number or a string; it doesn't matter.</p>
<p>If you're dealing with money, but don't want a leading dollar sign shown on the amount, you can also add this function, which uses the previous function but removes the <code>$</code>:</p>
<pre><code>function no$(dollarAmount)
{
    return $(dollarAmount).replace('$','');
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you're <strong>not</strong> dealing with money, and have varying decimal formatting requirements, here's a more versatile function:</p>
<pre><code>function addCommas(number, minDecimalPlaces = 0, maxDecimalPlaces = Math.max(3,minDecimalPlaces))
{
    const options = {};
    options.maximumFractionDigits = maxDecimalPlaces;
    options.minimumFractionDigits = minDecimalPlaces;
    return Intl.NumberFormat('en-US',options).format(number);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Oh, and by the way, the fact that this code does not work in some old version of Internet Explorer is completely intentional. I try to break Internet Explorer anytime that I can catch it not supporting modern standards.</p>

Q: How to recreate race condition with in a `callbackFlow`&#39;s `awaitClose` block? A: <p>The purpose of <code>callbackFlow</code> is to bring legacy code into the world of coroutines and Flows. You usually won't have any coroutine-aware code in the <code>callbackFlow</code>'s body.</p>
<p>For a <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> to occur you do need multiple threads running in parallel, so let's assume a legacy service that spawns its own thread to do whatever it needs to do. The typical pattern is that you, as the consumer of the service, need to register a listener to get notified of updates. To convert this pattern into a Flow is what <code>callbackFlow</code> is used for: When the listener is called, you send a new item into the Flow, and when the Flow is cancelled, you unregister the listener using <code>awaitClose</code>.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Now, for an example of how to create a <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> let's forgo creating an entire dummy service class with the boilerplate code to manage listeners. For demonstration purposes only it suffices to put the core logic directly into the <code>callbackFlow</code>:</p>
<pre><code>fun test(): Flow&lt;List&lt;Int&gt;&gt; = callbackFlow {
    // Usually you want to register a listener here and send a new item into the Flow when it is called.
    // For simplicity's sake we put the imaginary service's logic directly here:
    val list = mutableListOf&lt;Int&gt;()
    thread {
        while (true) {
            TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.sleep(10)
            repeat(100) { list.add(it) }
            trySend(list.toList())
        }
    }

    awaitClose {
        // Here you would usually clean up and unregister the listener since it isn't needed anymore.
        // Instead, we loop over the list until a ConcurrentModificationException stops us:
        println(&quot;CLOSE&quot;)
        while (true) {
            list.forEach {}
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Let's assume you collect the Flow like this:</p>
<pre><code>fun main() = runBlocking {
    var count = 0

    test().collect {
        println(&quot;received list #${++count} with length ${it.size}&quot;)
        if (count &gt;= 3) cancel()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The following output is produced:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>received list #1 with length 100
received list #2 with length 200
received list #3 with length 300
CLOSE
java.util.ConcurrentModificationException
    at java.util.ArrayList$Itr.next(ArrayList.java:860)
    ...
</code></pre>
<h3>How this works</h3>
<p><code>thread {}</code> is used here to create a separate thread, otherwise there is no way a <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> can occur. You could do this with coroutines as well by creating a new scope and switching to the IO dispatcher, for example. Let's call this new thread <strong>T2</strong>.</p>
<p>After <code>thread</code> starts the new thread T2 it immediately continues with the current thread, the one the <code>callbackFlow</code>'s body runs in. Let's call that <strong>T1</strong>. Both threads T1 and T2 run now in parallel.</p>
<p>While T2 starts executing its payload, the next thing to do for T1 is calling <code>awaitClose</code>. This suspends the current coroutine and will only start executing its body when the current coroutine is cancelled. That won't be the case for some time now, so the coroutine frees the thread T1, and only T2 is running. Well, there is <em>another</em> coroutine running in the <code>main()</code> function that collects the Flow, but that's not relevant here.</p>
<p>After T2 sent three lists to the Flow its collector decides to stop the Flow by cancelling the collection (<code>if (count &gt;= 3) cancel()</code>). T2 doesn't see this, however, and continues unimpeded. But the suspended <code>awaitClose</code> wakes up and continues, starting to execute its body. This is not guaranteed to use the previous thread T1 again (could be another thread from the same dispatcher), but for simplicity's sake let's assume it <em>is</em> the same thread T1. It certainly won't be T2, though, which is important, because these two threads will now run in parallel and compete for exclusive access to the list.</p>
<p>The body of <code>awaitClose</code> now repeatedly iterates over the list using thread T1. Meanwhile, T2 modifies the list at the same time. The list implementation detects that the the list was modified during an ongoing iteration and throws a <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> in T1.</p>
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<p>The body of the <code>callbackFlow</code> is expected to just set up the legacy API, usually by registering a listener. The rest of the time is spent suspending in <code>awaitClose</code>. The actual Flow emissions are executed asynchronously by another thread (or at least onther coroutine). After all, the listeners are called from the legacy API. And the thread that runs the legacy API is what can conflict with the thread that eventually executes the body of <code>awaitClose</code>.</p>
<p>This is why the documentation cautions you to make sure that what you put in the <code>awaitClose</code> body  (usually unregistering the listener) is actually thread-safe:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The callback <code>register</code>/<code>unregister</code> methods provided by an external API must be thread-safe, because <code>awaitClose</code> block can be called at any time due to asynchronous nature of cancellation, even concurrently with the call of the callback.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;callback&quot; here would be the part that is running in T2 from the example above.</p>
<hr />
<p>As a final note: There is another way you can run into a <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> here, namely, when you actually expose the internal <em>mutable</em> list to the outside by emitting it into the Flow. In the example above that cannot happen because <code>toList()</code> is called first on the mutable list creating an immutable copy. If this would be omitted then the collector of the Flow could be iterating over the list, which would then be <em>the exact same instance</em> as the internal mutable list, resulting in a <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>.</p>
<p>You should never put mutable data into a Flow, though. If you make sure to make everything immutable first (or at least create a copy/clone of it) as in the example above, then you are safe.</p>
